If you believe that, I have a bridge to sell to you. There are lots of
legends floating around about Japanese swords but they too have to
follow laws of physics, unfortunately. 

Mind you, I have with my own eyes examined high-quality Japanese
swords that had nicks on the blade. In fact, every wakizashi whose
blade was shown in the exhibition I visited last summer (there were
five or six of them) was nicked. They had generally 3-4 nicks halfway
up the blade. I tried to take a picture of them but it was too dark
for my digicam and flashes were forbidden. The katana blades were
intact. My hypothesis (I couldn't confirm it) was that the wakizashi
had been used in training fights but the katanas were only for show.
The swords belonged to the Imperial Russian collection and were
acquired by Czars in 18th-19th centuries, though some of them were old
by that time.

